# Character-Twisted Power Sums: An Interactive Tour

A periodic arithmetic mask can turn a long sum into a compact formula. Begin with the [Dirichlet character](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_character) modulo four, whose repeating weights are $1,0,-1,0$. The first widget exposes one period term by term.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The direct definition remains useful for experimentation.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## The linear collapse

Every complete block contributes $-2$, so $S_1(4q,x)=-2q$ regardless of $x$. Compare the exact evaluator with the horizontal-line visualization.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the complete linear proof</summary>
In block $j$, only positions $4j+1$ and $4j+3$ survive. Their contribution is $(x+4j+1)-(x+4j+3)=-2$. Summing $q$ blocks gives $-2q$. For $q>0$ this is negative, whereas every even power is nonnegative.
</details>

## The quadratic threshold

In degree two, difference of squares changes each block into $-4(x+4j+2)$. Summing the arithmetic progression gives $S_2(4q,x)=-4q(x+2q)$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the quadratic derivation and sign argument</summary>
The block is $(x+4j+1)^2-(x+4j+3)^2=-4(x+4j+2)$. Since $\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}(x+4j+2)=q(x+2q)$, the formula follows. If $q>0$ and $x>-2q$, both $q$ and $x+2q$ are positive, making the product negative and excluding every even power.
</details>

## Explore scale and sharpness

The heatmap shows the boundary $x=-2q$ across many parameter choices. The computational race illustrates why symbolic compression matters.

{{visualization:2}}

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal sharpness examples</summary>
At the boundary $x=-2q$, the quadratic sum is $0$, so strict inequality is essential. In the positive region, $q=1$ and $x=-3$ give $S_2(4,-3)=4=2^2$. Evenness of the exponent is also essential: $S_1(16,x)=-8=(-2)^3$.
</details>

For background on the untwisted setting, see [power sums](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faulhaber%27s_formula), and for the target values see [perfect powers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfect_power).

